# legacy-peer-deps for the mobile/ Expo app — verified, not assumed.
#
# Checked against Expo SDK 57 (expo@57.0.19, react-native@0.86.3, react@19.2.3).
# `npx expo install --check` reports "Dependencies are up to date", so the pinned
# versions already are the Expo-recommended set — there is no cleaner combination
# to move to.
#
# A strict install (npm default, legacy-peer-deps=false) does NOT hard-error, but
# it does not produce a clean tree either. Strict mode auto-installs optional peer
# deps, which drags react-native-reanimated@4.6.0 + react-native-worklets@0.12.1
# into the graph and then cannot satisfy this peer range:
#
#   npm warn While resolving: expo-modules-core@57.0.15
#   npm warn Found: react-native-worklets@0.12.1
#   npm warn Could not resolve dependency:
#   npm warn peerOptional react-native-worklets@"^0.7.4 || ^0.8.0 || ^0.9.0 || ^0.10.0"
#   npm warn   from expo-modules-core@57.0.15
#   npm warn Conflicting peer dependency: react-native-worklets@0.10.4
#
# It also pulls in react-dom@19.2.8 (a web renderer, mismatched from the pinned
# react@19.2.3) even though this slice has no web target. Pulling in Reanimated 4
# / Worklets just to appease strict resolution would be an unrelated change to the
# app's runtime architecture, which is out of scope here.
#
# The committed package-lock.json is resolved with this flag; `npm ci` only
# reproduces it when the flag is set. Without it: "npm error Missing:
# <optional-peer> from lock file".
#
# Scope: this file only affects installs run inside mobile/. The root Next.js
# project is unaffected.
legacy-peer-deps=true
